# Pinned from the injected red run of 2026-07-29 (see README.md in this
# directory for what this proof does and does not replay).
#
# Both tests measured a fixed amount of wall clock against work whose real cost
# is a timer, and a timer on a loaded host fires late.
#
#   dead_pid_aborts_joins_hung_producer_then_receiver_releases spent two real
#   DRIVER_RELEASE_TIMEOUTs (10.08s measured alone on an idle machine) inside a
#   12s assertion, a 1.19x margin.
#
#   replacement_start_waits_out_a_shutting_down_predecessor gave the replacement
#   a ceiling of DRAIN + 2 x 100ms starting ~375ms after the predecessor began,
#   so its entire margin against a 1200ms drain enforced by a timer was that
#   head start.
#
# The injection removes both fixes and makes each loss deterministic by adding
# the lateness the load supplied: 3s before the driver stop, 1.5s before the
# predecessor's post-drain teardown. Load produced these panics in some runs;
# the injection produces them in every run.
exit: 101

# The dead-pid teardown now costs 13s against the unchanged 12s bound. This is
# the panic measured under load, verbatim, at supervisor.rs:8382 (8077/8065 in
# older trees).
contains: did not release within 12s

# The replacement is refused while its predecessor is leaving normally — the
# TASK-ATAXN outage this test exists to keep fixed. The measured 2026-07-29
# panic named `waited 1.50062225s`; the injected one names `waited
# 1.402445459s`. The duration is the load-dependent part, so the signature is
# the assertion, not the number.
contains: a replacement start refused while its predecessor was shutting down

# The refusal must still be the departing-predecessor classification, not a
# different lock holder: pinning this keeps a green replay from being bought by
# some unrelated instance-lock failure.
contains: its own whole shutdown budget

# Both, not one: a single red would leave the other fix unproven, and the two
# defects are independent.
contains: 2 failed
